# Line endings: LF in the working tree, on every platform, and that is rule 12 rather than
# tidiness.
#
# `fingerprint_files` hashes the **bytes** of every file that shapes extraction -- the
# detectors' YAML, the rules loader, and both extractor trees -- and a cached fact is valid
# only if the tool that read it is unchanged. Git's default on Windows is `autocrlf=true`,
# which checks those same files out with CRLF: same commit, different bytes, different
# fingerprint. A map written by a checkout on one platform would then be discarded by a
# checkout of the *same commit* on the other as "written by a different tool", which is the
# refusal `resource_root()`'s layout exists to prevent, one platform over.
#
# It is not only the hash. `packaging/release.sh` is run by `/bin/sh`, which reads a CRLF
# line ending as part of the command on the line; and `extractors/python/extract.py` slices
# an expression out of a file on **byte** offsets (rule 75), which `ast` computes against
# the text as `read_text` hands it over -- universal newlines, so LF. The source this tool
# *reads* may hold anything and is translated on the way in; the source this tool *is* has
# one answer, and this file is where it is given.
#
# `text=auto` leaves git to decide what is text at all, so the 32 tracked images and icons
# are untouched. Every tracked text file is already LF in the index, so this changes nothing
# today and holds for the next clone.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Never translated, whatever git guesses. An icon or a screenshot with a byte rewritten is
# a corrupt file rather than a differently-spelled one.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.db    binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
